{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ULou2zj33181Ax6sqQiBwb","type":"tool_result","content":"  provider: 'auto', // or a concrete venue; defaults server-side\n  slippageBps: 50,\n});\n\nconst outcome = await client.swap(quotes[0]); // SubmitOutcome\n```\n\nMethod signatures:\n\n```ts\nclient.getSwapQuote(params: SwapQuoteParams): Promise<SwapQuote[]>;\nclient.swap(quote: SwapQuote, opts?: { autoTrustline?: boolean }): Promise<SubmitOutcome>;\n```\n\nThe standalone `quoteSwap(api, body): Promise<SwapQuoteContent>` export is available for advanced callers that already\nhold a `PollarApiClient`.\n\n---\n\n### Ramps\n\nA ramp is an order of one or more legs that Pollar runs server-side: the\nprovider's leg (fiat in or out), and a swap or a bridge when the asset or chain\nthe user wants is not the provider's. Pick a pair from the routes, quote it,\nturn the quote the user accepts into an order, and follow the order.\n\n```ts\nimport { describeRampAction, rampQuoteFields } from '@pollar/core';\n\nconst { routes } = await client.getRampRoutes('ONRAMP');\nconst { fiat, asset } = routes[0];\nconst { quotes, rejected } = await client.quoteRamp({\n  direction: 'ONRAMP',\n  fiat: { currency: fiat.currency, rail: fiat.rail, country: fiat.country },\n  asset: { code: asset.code, identifier: asset.identifier, chain: asset.chain },\n  amount: { value: '100', denomination: 'in' }, // 'out' fixes what the user receives\n});\n// Ask the user for rampQuoteFields(quotes[0]) and send the answers as `payee`.\nconst order = await client.createRampOrder({ quoteId: quotes[0].quoteId, payee: { name: 'Ana Perez' } });\nawait client.pollRampOrder(order.id, { onUpdate: (o) => render(describeRampAction(o)) });\n```\n\n- `pendingAction` says what the order waits for: `FIAT_PAYMENT` (a QR, already\n  rendered in `instructions.scannable`, or bank details), `SIGNATURE` (sign with\n  `signRampLeg(orderId, actionId)` from an explicit Authorize),\n  `COSIGN_APPROVAL` (vote on it like any co-sign request) or `KYC` (a link).\n- `nextPollAfter` is when to read the order again; `pollRampOrder` follows it\n  and stops once the order settles.\n- A failure carries `resolutionAction`; `rampResolutionText(failure)` turns it\n  into what the user can do. `rampLegTitle` and `rampLegStatus` describe each leg.\n- A payee the provider would refuse answers `400 VALIDATION_ERROR` with the\n  reason in `body.message`; a quote with no route answers `422\nSDK_RAMPS_NO_ROUTE` with `body.rejected`.\n- `listRampOrders({ pending: 'true' })` returns the orders waiting on the user.\n\n### Ramp adapters\n\nTo build a provider package for Pollar's backend, use `RampAdapter`, `defineRampAdapter` and `defineRampAdapterRegistration` from `@pollar/core` or `@pollar/core/ramps`. Install its server registration in the backend; its rails then appear in the routes above. Pollar retains credentials, persistence, lifecycle and reconciliation. See the [server registration guide](../../docs/development/custom-ramp-adapters.md), [provider implementation](../../examples/custom-ramp/adapter.ts) and [application example](../../examples/custom-ramp/client.ts).\n\n`RampChain` is a closed enum (`STELLAR`, `POLYGON`, `SOLANA`), independent of\nwallet networks. Use `RampChain.POLYGON` or `'POLYGON'` for a quote filter,\n65:> `submitCardKyc()`, `issueCard()`, `getCards()`, `getCardBalance()`, `getCardTransactions()`,\n93:> `WalletBalanceRecord.balance` and `.available` are `string | null` - `null` means the chain\n96:> Stellar), and balances gained `decimals`, `limit` and `sponsored`. `signTx` accepts\n168:- Fetch wallet balances via `PollarClient` (`refreshBalance()` / `getWalletBalance()`) - v2 balances are **multichain**,\n169:  tagged by `chain`; every chain reports its native coin plus each token the app enabled. A balance is `null` when the\n646:sponsored `createAccount` (the new account starts at \"0\" balance; the app's sponsor wallet pays the base reserve and\n694:### Balances & assets\n696:#### `client.refreshBalance(): Promise<void>`\n698:Refreshes the authenticated user's OWN multichain balances and pushes the result to `WalletBalanceState`\n699:(`onWalletBalanceStateChange`). No argument - always the current session's wallets. See the multichain notes on\n700:`WalletBalanceRecord` above (a `null` balance is an unreadable chain, not zero).\n702:#### `client.getWalletBalance(publicKey, network?): Promise<WalletBalanceContent>`\n704:General-purpose balance lookup for ANY wallet on ANY network - not scoped to this application, and it does not touch\n705:`WalletBalanceState`. Use `refreshBalance()` for the session's own wallet.\n816:lists a provider's vaults/pools with their live APY; `getEarnPosition()` returns the connected wallet's balance + APY\n995:const unsubBalance = client.onWalletBalanceStateChange((s) => {\n996:  /* balances */\n1012:`getWalletBalanceState()`, `getEnabledAssetsState()`, `getSessionsState()`, `getNetworkState()`, `getLogger()`.\n1033:Lightweight helper to submit a signed transaction straight to Horizon (bypassing the Pollar API). Balance fetching\n1034:lives on `PollarClient` now (`refreshBalance()` / `getWalletBalance(publicKey, network?)`).\n1152:  WalletBalanceRecord,\n1153:  WalletBalanceContent,\n1154:  WalletBalanceState,\n1224:  StellarBalance,\n36:> Earlier: **0.11.4**. **KYC by ramp corridor**: the v2 ramp quote\n40:> `submitProviderRegistration`); corridor-scoped KYC (`corridorId` on the status, providers and\n41:> resolve calls); typed KYC errors (`PollarApiError`), `KycStatus` gains `'expired'`, and\n42:> `pollKycDecision()` returns the settled read. **Behaviour change:** `pollKycStatus()` now also returns\n65:> `submitCardKyc()`, `issueCard()`, `getCards()`, `getCardBalance()`, `getCardTransactions()`,\n785:  `COSIGN_APPROVAL` (vote on it like any co-sign request) or `KYC` (a link).\n844:### KYC\n846:Fetch KYC status/providers and start or resolve a KYC flow. Pass `corridorId` when a ramp route asks for KYC: the\n847:read then answers for the corridor's KYC step, whichever of its options the user passed. With neither id,\n848:`getKycStatus()` answers for the app's own KYC steps and names the `pendingStep`.\n851:client.getKycStatus(providerId?: string, corridorId?: string): Promise<KycStatusContent>;\n852:client.getKycProviders(country: string, corridorId?: string): Promise<{ providers: KycProvider[] }>;\n853:client.startKyc(body: KycStartBody): Promise<KycStartResponse>;\n854:client.resolveKyc(providerId: string, level?: KycLevel, country?: string, corridorId?: string, idempotencyKey?: string);\n855:client.pollKycDecision(providerId: string, opts?: { intervalMs?: number; timeoutMs?: number; corridorId?: string }): Promise<KycStatusContent>;\n856:client.pollKycStatus(providerId: string, opts?: { intervalMs?: number; timeoutMs?: number; corridorId?: string }): Promise<KycStatus>;\n859:- `KycStatus` is `'none' | 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired'`. A read also carries `decisionStatus`\n862:- `pollKycDecision()` stops when the decision settles - approved, rejected, expired, held for manual review, or approved\n863:  by the vendor and still being recorded - and returns the whole read. `pollKycStatus()` is the same poll reduced to a\n865:- The status, providers and start calls throw `PollarApiError` with the backend code (`SDK_KYC_UNDER_REVIEW`,\n866:  `SDK_KYC_ALREADY_APPROVED`, ...). `resolveKyc()` treats `SDK_KYC_ALREADY_APPROVED` as approved, and reusing the same\n868:- `startKyc()` gets a 30 s budget instead of the 10 s request default, since creating a session waits on the vendor.\n872:A ramp corridor, and an app's own KYC, can require ordered steps: every step is required, in order, and each accepts any\n890:For a `KYC` step, open the option the step names (`optionId`) with the step's `corridorId`. The v2 quote also lists, in\n1196:  // KYC\n1197:  KycLevel,\n1198:  KycStatus,\n1199:  KycDecisionStatus,\n1200:  KycStatusContent,\n1201: 